+## TP2 tbstream
+
+The same two hosts linked by a direct USB4 cable through the Linux
+USB4STREAM character device instead of the ConnectX-3 cards (see
+[TP2.md](TP2.md) and [tools/tbstream/README.md](../../../tools/tbstream/README.md)).
+Development measurements, not published cells. Kernel 7.2.6 mainline,
+`thunderbolt_stream` on both Strix Halo hosts, one stream named `gufo`,
+defaults (ring_size 256, throttling 8192) unless noted, measured
+2026-09-30/10-01. Final cable: a certified passive TB5 cable (Cable
+Matters B0CP8K1C59) training Gen3, 40 Gb/s each way; the earlier
+Gen2 (20 Gb/s each way) rows came from an uncertified active cable whose
+Gen3-max retimer and one identity-unreadable chip capped the link — see
+the cable qualification row.
+
+| Experiment | Decision / evidence |
+| --- | --- |
+| Exchange latency (decode shape) | **p50 22–23 µs, p99 34–42 µs** (`--mode exchange --bytes 10240 --frames 2000 --noverify`, Gen3 cable, reproduced three times: 23.4/22.9, 23.3/22.1) — **at parity with the RDMA baseline of 24 µs**. The transport itself checksums nothing (the GPU adds the partials directly, as verbs does), and the first reported figure of 43.7 µs turned out to be ~21 µs of the probe's own byte-at-a-time payload checksum; `--noverify` skips it (integrity stays covered by checksummed runs, which reproduce at the slower figure). |
+| Cable qualification | A USB-IF/Intel certified passive TB5 cable (Cable Matters B0CP8K1C59) trains **Gen3, 40 Gb/s each way, on hot plug** — no cold start — with a clean retimer chain (one identified board PS8830 per host, no cable retimers). The prior uncertified "80G" cable carried a Gen3-max PS8830 plus an identity-unreadable chip, trained Gen2, and correlated with the wedge-era instability. Recorded as the required cable class. |
+| One-way bandwidth (prefill shape) | **1.01–1.03 GB/s at 1 MiB, 1.04–1.05 at 5 MiB, 1.13–1.14 at 32 MiB** (`--noverify`, Gen3) — flat across frame sizes, so the pace is the driver's ring drain, not the frames. Without `--noverify` the receiver's checksum halves these to 0.5–0.7 GB/s, which masked the real figures through the Gen2/Gen3 comparison (both were checksum-bound, so the "link rate doubled, bandwidth did not move" observation holds but at the higher base). The sender idles at ~0% CPU; the remaining gap to RDMA's 3.27 GB/s is the drain path (interrupt-paced). A 5 MiB prefill exchange takes ~5 ms against RDMA's 1.9 ms. |
+| Latency/bandwidth trade-off | The stream's `throttling` knob batches the transmit path: at Gen2 (checksummed probe) default 8192 gave 43.6 µs / 0.50 GB/s versus ~110 µs / 0.77 GB/s at 65536. At Gen3 the tuned value gains nothing (0.48 vs 0.51, checksummed) — the host path saturates first — so serving keeps the default. Both figures predate `--noverify`; re-sweeps belong to the interrupt-throttle work. |
+| Ring capacity | `--mode exchange --bytes 33554432` (both ranks write their 32 MiB frame, then read) deadlocks both ends inside their write deadlines: no rank drains while the other writes. The transport's always-draining reader thread is required, not a refinement, for frames near or above the device's rings. |
+| Read-path requirement | Raw device experiments: a frame delivered while no reader is parked in a blocking `read(2)` is invisible to `poll(2)` and to nonblocking reads; a plain blocking read returns it at once, and an empty ring reads as zero bytes, not end of file. The transport therefore reads on a second, blocking descriptor (never closed; the reader is detached at stop), spins the exchange window before sleeping on empty reads, and keeps the nonblocking descriptor for deadline-bounded writes. |
+| Open-before-write ordering | Data a rank writes before its peer opened the device is stranded: the probe pairs a post-open barrier, and the transport's bootstrap readiness exchange plays that role after its reader starts. |
+| Driver robustness | External kills of a blocked writer wedge the stream (open then fails EIO, teardown hangs, and a wedged link flaps every ~55 s until the streams are removed); a clean exit through the write deadline does not. Recovery ladder: remove the stream on both ends, then PCI-rebind the controller, then reboot. Echo-mode (`--mode ping`) at 10 KiB still stalls after all fixes; the exchange and smoke patterns — the shapes the transport uses — are unaffected. |
+| Bandwidth plateau (root-caused) | Neither per-stream knob moves the ~1.1 GB/s plateau: `throttling` 0 and 65536, `ring_size` 256 and 1024 all measure 1.08–1.14 GB/s. Source reading of 7.2.6 found the real structure: the NHI ring descriptor's size field is 12 bits, so **every byte moves as ≤4 KiB frames, one descriptor each** (1.1 GB/s ≈ 270K frames/s), the stream rings use end-to-end flow control, and **RX buffers are reposted one at a time from the reader's `read()` syscall** (`tbstream_dev_consume_rx` in process context) with a per-frame `wake_up` — a latency-bound serial pipeline. Receiver measurements during a run agree: **~125K interrupts/s and no core above 20% busy** — not CPU-bound, not window-depth-bound (why `ring_size` changes nothing), paced by the per-frame interrupt→wake→copy→repost loop. Path credits (`dma_credits` ≤ router-reported 14) may bound the ceiling further up, but the software pipeline binds first. The L1T 78 Gb/s custom-driver claim is the existence proof that the fabric itself goes far faster with bigger logical transfers and kernel-side repost. |
+| Transport survey (2026-10-02) | Alternatives measured on the same pair/cable: **thunderbolt-net** (in-kernel IP over USB4, MTU 9000, `pm_qos_resume_latency_us=100` which drops ping RTT from ~600 µs to **117 µs**): iperf3 one-way **9.27 Gbit/s (1.16 GB/s)**, no parallel-stream scaling, bidirectional **31.7 Gbit/s aggregate** (RX rail reached 21.2 Gbit/s). So the per-direction IP ceiling ≈ tbstream's 1.13-1.16 GB/s on AMD↔AMD; the fabric itself exceeds it when driven bidirectionally. **hellas-ai/thunderbolt-ibverbs** (RDMA-over-USB4, claims ~48 Gb/s/dir 4-HCA + 7 µs latency) was built (v0.3.4+2449afd, kernel 7.2.6) and brought up: native rail handshake completes, `usb4_rdma{0,5}` register, GIDs populate via `roce_netdev=thunderbolt0`, but every RC WRITE stalls partially-delivered (sender retransmit loop `reason=1 tx_pending=0`, receiver discards ~96% of the first WRITE at QP teardown) at all sizes 4 KiB-1 MiB — an upstream data-path bug on our AMD↔AMD single-cable pair; issue drafted at `/root/tbv-issue.md` with both hosts' dmesg. The stock tbstream module stays in service; the patched stream module remains parked at `/root/tb-stream-module/` (parity, no gain, launch-order sensitive). |
+| thunderbolt-ibverbs root-caused + fixed (2026-10-02) | The zero-completions failure was a **mixed-E2E bug**: `tbv_native_e2e_auto_enabled()` returns true when `xd->tb` is unresolved at rail creation, so one host's path got E2E rings (`tx_flags=0x6`) while the peer's stayed non-E2E (`0x2`) — visible as differing `path_cfg` lines in debugfs `peers`. The E2E host's TX ring then posts descriptors that never transmit (its ACKs cannot return), stalling every RC WRITE. **Fix: `native_e2e=0` on both hosts** (no code). Measured on our pair: ib_write_lat **8.2/9.2 µs min/avg at 2 B, 15/16.3 µs at 10 KiB** (vs tbstream 22 µs RTT); ib_write_bw **1095 MiB/s sustained at 64 KiB**, 734 MiB/s at 1 MiB (software-retry tax: ~8% data-frame loss without E2E). Upstream issue with root cause + measurements drafted at `/root/tbv-issue.md`. Symmetric `native_e2e=1` wedges on this NHI (as their comment warns) — software-credit mode is the AMD answer. |
+| gufo over usb4_rdma (2026-10-02 capstone) | **End-to-end TP2 serving works**: gufo `tp2-rdma` rebuilt against their `rdma-core-usb4` (two one-line link-layer relaxations in verbs.cpp — the transport assumed InfiniBand link layer; the device reports Ethernet/RoCE), both ranks load, pair, and generate **correct output** over verbs-on-USB4 (`--tp-transport rdma --tp-device 0 --tp-gid-index 1`). Performance is not competitive yet: MTP decode **38.6 tok/s vs 76.1 over tbstream** (96% draft acceptance), because their transport's RPC-unfriendly flow control shows up under gufo's exchange pattern: ~8x frame amplification from whole-message retransmits (retry timeout ~75 ms) plus 230K `data_tx_credit_stalls` (start-of-message credit reservation of 32 frames/message drains the 768-credit window under queued exchanges). perftest over the same device: 16 µs @10 KiB, 1095 MiB/s @64 KiB — the verbs path is sound; the credit/retransmit tuning needs upstream work (or a second-rail striping setup) before it can serve. tbstream stays the production transport. |
