+**Transports.** Both transports implement the same `Communicator` exchange:
+stage the partial into a send window, move one framed record per exchange
+(header plus partial, into the peer's next rotating receive window), and
+let the GPU add the peer's partial straight from its window. RDMA does this
+with one write-with-immediate, whose completion is the arrival signal.
+`tbstream` replaces the network stack with the USB4STREAM character device
+of a Linux 7.2+ `thunderbolt_stream` driver: the device is an ordered byte
+stream in both directions, so a background thread writes each frame while
+a reader thread, always draining, places the peer's frame of the same
+exchange into the window and raises the arrival flag. The reader owns every
+read because a rank writes its frame before reading the peer's; without it,
+two ranks whose frames exceed the device's rings could each block writing
+into the other's full receive ring. The framed records, rotating windows,
+scope and ordinal checks, staged ready/arrived flags and GPU wait kernels
+are the verbs transport's, unchanged. The bootstrap socket stays open under
+the same liveness rule: readable means the peer is gone. A lost stream or a
+pulled cable surfaces as a read or write deadline within the collective
+timeout.
+
